Great! Here's a simple HTML/CSS example of a responsive navigation menu with a hamburger toggle. This snippet includes the structure and some basic styling.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Responsive Navigation</title>
 <style>
 body {
 font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
 margin: 0;
 padding: 0;
 }
 .nav-bar {
 display: flex;
 justify-content: space-between;
 align-items: center;
 background-color: #333;
 padding: 10px;
 }
 .nav-bar .logo {
 color: #fff;
 font-size: 24px;
 text-decoration: none;
 }
 .nav-bar .menu {
 display: none;
 flex-direction: column;
 }
 .nav-bar .menu a {
 color: #fff;
 padding: 10px;
 text-decoration: none;
 }
 .hamburger {
 display: block;
 cursor: pointer;
 background: none;
 border: none;
 color: #fff;
 font-size: 24px;
 }
 @media (min-width: 768px) {
 .nav-bar .menu {
 display: flex;
 flex-direction: row;
 }
 .hamburger {
 display: none;
 }
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <nav class="nav-bar">
 <a href="#" class="logo">Logo</a>
 <button class="hamburger" onclick="toggleMenu()">☰</button>
 <div class="menu" id="menu">
 <a href="#home">Home</a>
 <a href="#services">Services</a>
 <a href="#about">About</a>
 <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
 </div>
 </nav>

 <script>
 function toggleMenu() {
 const menu = document.getElementById('menu');
 if (menu.style.display === 'flex') {
 menu.style.display = 'none';
 } else {
 menu.style.display = 'flex';
 }
 }
 </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This example uses a simple JavaScript function to toggle the display of the menu when the hamburger icon is clicked. You can customize the styles further to match your website's design.
