Absolutely! Creating a responsive navigation menu is a great way to ensure your website is accessible and user-friendly across all devices. Here's a simple HTML and CSS example demonstrating a collapsible navigation menu with a hamburger icon for smaller screens:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Responsive Navigation Menu</title>
 <style>
 body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
 .navbar { background-color: #333; overflow: hidden; }
 .navbar a { float: left; display: block; color: #f2f2f2; text-align: center; padding: 14px 16px; text-decoration: none; }
 .navbar a:hover { background-color: #ddd; color: black; }
 .hamburger-icon { display: none; font-size: 30px; color: white; cursor: pointer; padding: 14px 16px; }
 .navbar-links { display: flex; }
 .navbar-links a { flex-grow: 1; }
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
 .navbar-links { display: none; }
 .hamburger-icon { display: block; }
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <nav class="navbar">
 <div class="hamburger-icon" onclick="toggleMenu()">☰</div>
 <div class="navbar-links" id="navLinks">
 <a href="#home">Home</a>
 <a href="#services">Services</a>
 <a href="#about">About</a>
 <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
 </div>
 </nav>

 <script>
 function toggleMenu() {
 var x = document.getElementById("navLinks");
 if (x.style.display === "flex") {
 x.style.display = "none";
 } else {
 x.style.display = "flex";
 }
 }
 </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example:
- The navigation menu (`.navbar`) contains links that are displayed horizontally on larger screens.
- The `.hamburger-icon` is displayed only on smaller screens (when the width is 600px or less).
- Clicking the hamburger icon toggles the visibility of the navigation links using a simple JavaScript function.

This ensures that your site remains easy to navigate on both desktop and mobile devices.

