Absolutely! Lazy loading images is a great way to enhance your website's performance by deferring the loading of off-screen images until they are needed. Here's a simple implementation:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Lazy Load Example</title>
 <style>
 .image-container {
 width: 100%;
 height: 400px;
 background-color: #f0f0f0;
 display: flex;
 justify-content: center;
 align-items: center;
 overflow: hidden;
 }
 img {
 max-width: 100%;
 height: auto;
 transition: opacity 0.3s ease-in-out;
 opacity: 0;
 }
 img[data-loaded='true'] {
 opacity: 1;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

 <section class="image-container">
 <img data-src="high-resolution-image.jpg" alt="Sample Image" class="lazy-load">
 </section>

 <script>
 document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
 const lazyImages = document.querySelectorAll('img.lazy-load');

 const imageObserver = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {
 entries.forEach(entry => {
 if (entry.isIntersecting) {
 const img = entry.target;
 img.src = img.getAttribute('data-src');
 img.setAttribute("data-loaded", "true");
 observer.unobserve(img);
 }
 });
 });

 lazyImages.forEach(img => {
 imageObserver.observe(img);
 });
 });
 </script>


</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
1. **HTML Structure**: The image is initially set with a placeholder and a `data-src` attribute holding the URL of the image.
2. **CSS Styling**: We use CSS to ensure that the images are styled properly and have a transition effect.
3. **JavaScript Logic**:
 - We use the `IntersectionObserver` API to detect when the image is about to enter the viewport.
 - Once it's about to appear, we replace the `data-src` attribute with the actual `src` attribute to load the image.
 - Finally, a transition effect is applied to smoothly display the image.

This approach helps in reducing the initial load time of your webpage and improves the user experience by only loading the images when they are needed.

