Certainly! A responsive navigation menu that includes skip links can significantly enhance accessibility by allowing users to bypass repetitive navigation links and directly access the main content. Here's an example of how you can create such a menu using HTML and CSS.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Responsive Navigation with Skip Links</title>
 <style>
 /* Basic reset for consistent styling */
 body, h1, ul, li, a {
 margin: 0;
 padding: 0;
 list-style: none;
 text-decoration: none;
 }
 /* Style for skip link */
 .skip-link {
 position: absolute;
 top: -40px;
 left: 0;
 background: #000;
 color: #fff;
 padding: 8px;
 z-index: 100;
 transition: top 0.3s;
 }
 .skip-link:focus {
 top: 10px;
 }
 /* Navigation styles */
 .nav-menu {
 background-color: #333;
 overflow: hidden;
 }
 .nav-menu ul {
 display: flex;
 justify-content: space-around;
 padding: 10px 0;
 }
 .nav-menu a {
 color: white;
 padding: 10px;
 }
 /* Responsive design */
 @media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
 .nav-menu ul {
 flex-direction: column;
 display: none;
 }
 .nav-menu.active ul {
 display: flex;
 }
 }
 /* Toggle button for mobile */
 .toggle-btn {
 display: none;
 background-color: #333;
 color: white;
 padding: 10px;
 cursor: pointer;
 }
 @media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
 .toggle-btn {
 display: block;
 }
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>

<nav class="nav-menu">
 <button class="toggle-btn" aria-label="Toggle navigation">Menu</button>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="#home">Home</a></li>
 <li><a href="#about">About</a></li>
 <li><a href="#services">Services</a></li>
 <li><a href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
 </ul>
</nav>

<main id="main">
 <h1>Welcome to Our Website</h1>
 <p>This is the main content of the page. Use the navigation menu to explore different sections.</p>
</main>

<script>
 const toggleBtn = document.querySelector('.toggle-btn');
 const navMenu = document.querySelector('.nav-menu');

 toggleBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
 navMenu.classList.toggle('active');
 });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- **Skip Link**: An anchor link at the top of the page that allows users to quickly access the main content. It becomes visible when focused.
- **Responsive Design**: The menu adapts to different screen sizes. It collapses into a toggleable menu on small screens.
- **Accessibility**: The skip link and responsive navigation provide better access to content for all users.

