Certainly! Creating responsive tables involves using a combination of flexible CSS styles and semantic HTML to ensure the table adapts to various screen sizes. Here's a simple example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Responsive Table Example</title>
 <style>
 .responsive-table {
 width: 100%;
 border-collapse: collapse;
 margin: 20px 0;
 }
 .responsive-table th, .responsive-table td {
 border: 1px solid #ddd;
 padding: 8px;
 text-align: left;
 }
 .responsive-table th {
 background-color: #f4f4f4;
 }
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
 .responsive-table thead {
 display: none;
 }
 .responsive-table tr {
 display: block;
 margin-bottom: 10px;
 }
 .responsive-table td {
 display: block;
 text-align: right;
 border: none;
 }
 .responsive-table td::before {
 content: attr(data-label);
 float: left;
 font-weight: bold;
 }
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <table class="responsive-table">
 <thead>
 <tr>
 <th>Name</th>
 <th>Age</th>
 <th>Occupation</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr>
 <td data-label="Name">Alice Johnson</td>
 <td data-label="Age">29</td>
 <td data-label="Occupation">Software Engineer</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td data-label="Name">Bob Smith</td>
 <td data-label="Age">34</td>
 <td data-label="Occupation">Data Analyst</td>
 </tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Points:
- **Use `@media` Queries:** These allow you to specify styles for different screen sizes. In this example, the table headers are hidden on screens smaller than 600px, and each cell behaves like a block element.
- **Data Attributes:** The `data-label` attribute is used to include the column name before each cell on smaller screens, enhancing readability.
- **Flexible Width:** Set the table width to `100%` to make it stretch across the available space.

