 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="dropdown-container">
 <label for="main-category">Main Category:</label>
 <select id="main-category" name="main-category">
 <option value="">Select a category</option>
 <option value="fruits">Fruits</option>
 <option value="vegetables">Vegetables</option>
 </select>
</div>

<div class="dropdown-container">
 <label for="sub-category">Sub Category:</label>
 <select id="sub-category" name="sub-category">
 <option value="">Select a sub category</option>
 </select>
</div>

<script>
 const mainCategory = document.getElementById('main-category');
 const subCategory = document.getElementById('sub-category');

 const subOptions = {
 fruits: ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Orange'],
 vegetables: ['Carrot', 'Broccoli', 'Spinach']
 };

 mainCategory.addEventListener('change', function() {
 const category = mainCategory.value;
 const options = subOptions[category] || [];

 // Clear existing options
 subCategory.innerHTML = '<option value="">Select a sub category</option>';

 // Add new options
 options.forEach(option => {
 const opt = document.createElement('option');
 opt.value = option.toLowerCase();
 opt.textContent = option;
 subCategory.appendChild(opt);
 });
 });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- **HTML Structure**: We have two dropdown menus, one for the main category and another for the sub-category.
- **CSS Styling**: Simple styling is applied to give it a clean look.
- **JavaScript Functionality**: When the main category is changed, the sub-category dropdown is populated based on the selection.

